    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I was reading something about how certain \"ghostly\" or non-harmonic components can sometimes appear in the spectral analysis of woodwinds. How do those non-integer partials or irregular overtones affect the perceived texture of the sound?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Indeed, 'ghostly' non-harmonic partials or irregular overtones can emerge from complex interactions within the air column of a woodwind instrument, particularly if there is some sort of non-ideal embouchure or playing technique. These can lead to inharmonicity — deviations from whole number harmonics that contribute to a more complex and sometimes dissonant overtone structure. Such inharmonicities are not prominent in classical instruments and more likely an attribute of electronic instruments or precise experimental setups. In the context of a wind instrument, they can give rise to subtle textures and colorations, detuning consonant harmonics slightly, but generally, these effects are more pronounced and intentional when exploring the physical boundaries of instrument acoustics.",
      "source": "target",
